
Adversary Intelligence Analysis: Structured Threat Assessment and Reporting

Introduction
This project applies the full intelligence lifecycle to analyze adversary behavior, assess threat activity, and produce structured, actionable intelligence reporting. The goal is to demonstrate practical competency in cyber threat intelligence (CTI), including collection, triage, analysis, and dissemination of findings relevant to modern threat actors and their techniques.
The assessment focuses on identifying adversary motivations, capabilities, and TTPs using open source intelligence, dark web ecosystem research, and technical indicators. Findings are evaluated using established analytic frameworks, including MITRE ATT&CK and structured analytic techniques, to ensure clarity, rigor, and repeatability.
This report is designed to reflect real world intelligence workflows used by security teams and organizations such as CrowdStrike. It emphasizes analytic reasoning, evidence based conclusions, and communication tailored for both technical and executive audiences. The objective is to transform raw data and observations into meaningful intelligence that supports decision making, strengthens defensive posture, and enhances understanding of adversary operations.

Executive Summary
	Finger Lakes Community Bank experienced a cyber incident on May 25th. As the financial industry continues its rapid digital transformation, cybersecurity has emerged as a critical priority for banks (Fortifying Banking Cybersecurity: Strategies for a Resilient Future, 2025). The banks leadership had just recently realized that cybersecurity was something they needed to think about. They had plans such as incident response, and disaster recovery plans as well as email policies in place but these plans were not enforced. Due to the lack of enforcement, the hacker was able to breach systems and initiate a fund transfer. The following is a plan that I have created that will help the bank more effectively detect and respond to this type of incident in the future. 
Overall Plan
	There are three important things that need to be addressed. They are employee awareness, device protection, and email security.  Employees will receive training on how to identify phishing emails and on their overall cybersecurity role in the company. Enhanced monitoring and endpoint detection will allow the bank to detect malicious activity early and meet regulations for safeguarding customer data. This plan is designed to identify threats sooner and deal with them before they can cause any damage.
Key takeaways
	Since a lot of banking has been changing to be more digital, they are more likely to be targeted by hackers. Finger Lakes community bank had a security breach because they didn’t enforce the policies that could have prevented them. It serves as a reminder that robust cybersecurity measures are not merely an option but an absolute necessity in today’s digital landscape (Key Lessons from 5 Cyber Attacks Every Business Should Know – Nucleo Consulting, 2023). 
	The most important takeaway is that fund transfers should never be approved solely over email. The policy of having handwritten approval needs to be done every time.  Another key takeaway is that monitoring and logging are essential. If you are monitoring suspicious activity you can respond to it immediately upon detection. The final takeaway that I think is very important is that endpoint compromise needs to be treated as being very serious. As soon as malware was found on the clerk’s laptop the bank should assume that sensitive data has probably been compromised.
Operationalize
	The first step to operationalization is to create security policies and have the security team train on the policies multiple times. The team needs to have clear rules about when to escalate threats, how to escalate them, and who they should be escalated to. There needs to be clear containment measures that the team will take when finding malware. The bank also needs to implement plans to review and update policies on a regular basis.

Legal, Regulatory, and Policy Compliance
Approach of detection
	Banks store and process sensitive financial information such as account numbers and social security numbers. This means that it is subject to very strict regulations. They are expected to maintain strong monitoring, logging and detection abilities. The following standard is critical to keeping credit card information safe.  This standard, established by the Payment Card Industry Security Standards Council (PCI DSS), ensures that any business handling credit card information implements secure processes for processing, storing, and transmitting data (Andrade, 2025). Financial institutions are also expected to follow the cybersecurity requirements under the Gramm-leach-Bliley Act (GLBA). GLBA requires banks to protect customer information with access controls and risk management.
	Keeping the logs from cyber incidents is a requirement so that investigators can review them after the incident. In the scenario there were repeated connections from XYZ free Hosting IP. These connections should have triggered alerts because they could be thought of as reconnaissance activity. If there had been an alert, then the hackers may never have gained access to the bank’s sensitive information.
Approach to response
	Legal and regulatory requirements strongly influence how Finger Lakes Bank must respond. Since there was unauthorized access, financial fraud, and a malware infection, the bank must respond in a way that preserves evidence and makes sure that reporting requirements are met. This means they must keep any evidence that could be needed for legal action. From a regulatory perspective, banks have to respond quickly and document all actions that happen during an incident to demonstrate due diligence.
	In the scenario the CFO’s email account is compromised. Any transactions that happened through the account during this time period need to be thought of as suspicious and should be thoroughly investigated. There should also be other steps taken such as credential resets and containment actions. Regulations may also require that customers be informed if any of their information was exposed.
Method of resolving gaps
	Finger Lakes Bank has several policies in place such as email policies and incident response plans. The gaps they have are in enforcement, monitoring, and execution. The first thing they should do to try and resolve gaps is to look at the policies are procedures they currently have. They should make sure that these policies include things that deal with such things as phishing awareness. By reviewing what they currently say they can bring them up to date. 
	Once things have been reviewed and updated, they can work on the next step which is to enforce the policies. The policy of needing written approval instead of just email approval being enforced would have stopped a fraudulent funds transfer.  The remote access policy also needs to be enforced to prevent unauthorized access through infected endpoints.
	Another way to resolve gaps is to have training for all staff members. Cybersecurity needs to involve all employees and not just people in IT. There should be regular email phishing training. The team that oversees fund transfers should also have training to watch out for fraud. The final step for resolving gaps is to keep watching for them and updating policies and procedures to deal with any new gaps that appear. 



