Countermeasures Analysis
Systems
	There are a lot of systems that can be put in place to reduce the negative impact from attacks on organizational systems.  Blocking suspicious IP addresses will slow down hackers by stopping things like brute force attacks from those addresses. Another thing that should be done is to make sure that an Incident response system is checking for and stopping all suspicious types of activity. 
A lot of attacks come in the form of phishing emails. A system that can stop this kind of thing is advanced email filtering. This will reduce negative impacts by keeping a lot of phishing emails away from people or warning people that the emails look suspicious. The final system to use would be to deploy endpoint detection and response tools. These focus on behaviors that seem suspicious such as malware or ransomware doing things and stops it.
Operations
	One of the best operational countermeasures is having multi-factor authentication required for each account. This way even if a hacker has your password, they will still need access to your phone or email address to get a security code before they can login to your account. This added step will slow down hackers hopefully enough for someone to realize it is suspicious such as someone logging in from an unknown location. Regular patching is another operation that should be done so that any known threats can be blocked.
	Hardening systems by removing unused software is also a good thing to do. Unused software is probably not being patched regularly because it may be forgotten about. This reduces the attack surface as well so that a hacker has less chances of finding a weak spot. Having an offline place to backup important data is also helpful. If the data, you need to run your business gets affected by ransomware it won’t stop your business from working since you have that information in a safe place. You should also have operational policies and procedures in place that are regularly updated. Hackers are constantly learning new methods to try and breach your security and the best way to stop it is to also keep changing how you approach security. When the policies and procedures are evolving at the same time as the attack methods then hackers will be less likely to breach the network.
Personnel
	A great personnel countermeasure to use is training employees on how to identify phishing emails. Human error is the reason a lot of cyber security breaches happen. When people know what to watch for, they are less likely to be fooled. Another personnel countermeasure that can reduce the negative impact of attacks is to have a team that is trained to deal with the attacks. Each person should know what they are expected to do and who to report to. By having these people ready it will dramatically reduce the amount of time a hacker has access to a network. 
	Personnel should also know how to come up with passwords that are both unique and strong. This means that they are long enough and use special characters as well as upper-case and lower-case letters and numbers. When passwords are strong then it would take a computer much longer to guess them. Personnel should also be sure to keep their login credentials safe by not writing them down or sharing them with anyone or entering them on untrusted websites. 
	






